{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Or has any god ever tried to come and take for Himself a nation from within a nation — by trials.</b> These are tests, as Moshe said to Pharaoh (Exodus 8): ״התפאר עלי״. <b>By signs.</b> Signs with the staff, in the manner of (Exodus 4): ״מזה בידך״. <b>And wonders.</b> These are the ten plagues. <b>And by war.</b> At the sea, as it says (Exodus 14): ״כי ה׳ נלחם להם״, or regarding the plague of the firstborn and regarding the tribes that He did to the gods of Egypt. <b>And by a strong hand.</b> That they went out with a high hand. <b>And by an outstretched arm.</b> In the pillar of fire and cloud. <b>And by great terrors.</b> The splitting of the Sea of Reeds for Israel and the drowning of the Egyptians in it. <b>In Egypt, before your eyes.</b> What he mentioned 'before your eyes' — the way of Scripture is sometimes to speak in plural language and immediately revert to singular language, and like it (Leviticus 19): ״ובקצרכם את קציר ארצכם לא תכלה״. But it appears to me that perhaps he mentioned 'before your eyes' so that the verse would begin with אל\"ף and end with כ\"ף, which is the numerical value of the name אהיה, for with this name Moshe was sent to Israel — the attribute of judgment that is within the attribute of mercy, an outstretched arm with great terrors, and the purpose of this exodus was for the sake of the Torah; therefore Scripture attached to it: ״אתה הראת לדעת״ and so on, ״מן השמים השמיעך את קולו״. And in this verse you will find twenty-two letters of the alphabet, and so you will find in the Ten Commandments all the letters of the alphabet, beginning with אל\"ף — ״אנכי״ — and ending with כ\"ף — ״לרעך״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:34.245729214Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}